Failure 1: "Status expected:<422> but was:<200>" — the pre-quarantine at exactly T0+6h returned 200, meaning the window check passed. Why? `!nowInstant.isBefore(deadline)` should be true at exactly 6h → 422. Unless declaredAt wasn't T0... 

Ah wait — I see. In `resetState` (@BeforeEach) clock is set to T0. But test execution order within the test: declare "B-6H-1" at T0, then `clock.set(T0+6h-1s)`, preQuarantine OK. Then `declareOk("d-2", "B-6H-2", ...)` — this declare happens at clock time T0+6h-1s! So B-6H-2's declaredAt = T0+6h-1s. Then `clock.set(T0+6h)` — that's only 1 second after declaration → within window → 200. My test bug, not code bug.

Fix: set clock back to T0 before declaring B-6H-2. Restructure:

```java
declareOk("d-1", "B-6H-1", ...);
clock.set(T0.plus(SIX_HOURS).minusSeconds(1));
preQuarantineOk("B-6H-1", "pq-1");

clock.set(T0);
declareOk("d-2", "B-6H-2", ...);
clock.set(T0.plus(SIX_HOURS));
→ 422
redeclare → declaredAt == T0+6h
preQuarantineOk at T0+6h → OK (now < declaredAt+6h)
```

Failure 2: PAUSED rolled back due to exception → fix with REQUIRES_NEW pause.

Fix service: inject PlatformTransactionManager, create TransactionTemplate with PROPAGATION_REQUIRES_NEW, use in doJudge for pause.

Edits:

BatchService constructor: add PlatformTransactionManager txManager; create `this.pauseTemplate = new TransactionTemplate(txManager); pauseTemplate.setPropagationBehavior(TransactionDefinition.PROPAGATION_REQUIRES_NEW);`

In doJudge missing branch:
```java
pauseTemplate.executeWithoutResult(s -> repo.transitionStatus(...));
throw ApiException.conflict(...);
```

Imports: org.springframework.transaction.PlatformTransactionManager, org.springframework.transaction.support.TransactionTemplate, org.springframework.transaction.TransactionDefinition.

